WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:29.920
Tenguq refers to the source of everything in the universe.

00:29.920 --> 00:36.480
and the universal law in which everything continuously goes through cycles of creation and destruction.

00:36.480 --> 00:41.680
Pumse Teguk symbolizes the root principle of universal philosophy

00:41.680 --> 00:48.000
and is constructed by applying the truth inherent in Teguk's yin and yang principles.

00:48.000 --> 00:52.400
Teguk Iljang stands for Gun of the Eight Trigrams

00:52.400 --> 00:59.280
and symbolizes the sky which embodies the concept of birth and beginnings and the root of all things.

00:59.360 --> 01:04.800
Pumse Teguk Iljang is the first thing you learn when you begin your Taekwondo training.

01:04.800 --> 01:08.000
It is designed for the exertion of only a little force,

01:08.000 --> 01:11.600
even beginners not used to these moves will be able to master it.

01:12.320 --> 01:16.480
Teguk Iljang also includes methods on how to complete movements

01:16.480 --> 01:21.200
and to change direction by stepping or turning around the Pumse progress line.

01:21.200 --> 01:25.520
We will now take a look at the Pumse of Teguk Iljang.

01:51.200 --> 02:09.680
Teguk Iljang consists of nine key moves namely

02:21.440 --> 02:35.440
These are the basics to the nine key moves of Teguk Iljang.

02:36.560 --> 02:40.800
We will now take a closer look at each of the key moves of Teguk Iljang.

02:44.160 --> 02:50.000
Begins with Moa Seogi where you can stand and move your left foot about a foot's length to your left.

02:52.160 --> 02:58.160
Breathe in, open your fists and raise them from the lower abdomen up to the solar plexus.

03:00.000 --> 03:04.160
Breathe out, then lower your fists back to the lower abdomen.

03:05.520 --> 03:10.240
Your face, torso and lower body should be balanced along the center line

03:11.920 --> 03:14.640
and your weight should be placed evenly on both feet.

03:16.080 --> 03:19.840
Both elbows should be bent like the internal angles of a hexagram

03:20.560 --> 03:23.920
and your fists should be placed apart about a fist's length.

03:25.600 --> 03:31.120
Retain 30% of your breath in the lower abdomen once the preparatory move is complete

03:31.840 --> 03:34.800
and be careful not to raise or tense your shoulders.

03:36.320 --> 03:42.400
Aremaki is a technique used to block an opponent's attack towards the lower body with your outer wrist.

03:43.200 --> 03:48.880
The blocking arm's elbow should be straightened in a way that positions your fist above your knees

03:48.960 --> 03:54.320
about two fists length and the wrist on the supporting arm should be straightened

03:54.320 --> 03:58.960
so that the back of your hand faces down and the wrist touches above the waist.

04:01.280 --> 04:07.360
The torso and arm should spin simultaneously and the blocking arm's elbow should not move

04:07.360 --> 04:14.640
in a straight line. Jiragi. Jiragi is a technique used to attack an opponent's torso

04:14.640 --> 04:20.000
by thrusting with the fist. The thrusting arm should be straightened and your fist should form

04:20.000 --> 04:26.640
a straight line with the target's philtrum, solar plexus, lower abdomen, etc. The wrist of your

04:26.640 --> 04:31.680
supporting arm should be straightened so that the back of your hand faces down and the wrist

04:31.680 --> 04:38.960
touches above the waist. The thrusting arm should move straight forward, slightly brushing the waist.

04:38.960 --> 04:43.680
Your elbow should not open outwards and the supporting arm should be pulled back,

04:43.680 --> 04:48.800
slightly brushing your waist as well. The supporting arm should not be pulled back

04:48.800 --> 04:54.800
excessively, pulling the shoulder along with it. The spin of the thrusting wrist should complete

04:54.800 --> 05:00.240
once your fist makes contact with the target. It is important to keep a straight line when

05:00.240 --> 05:06.400
punching a target with your fist. Anmakki is a technique used to defend from an opponent's

05:06.400 --> 05:13.760
outside attack using the outer wrist. The outer wrist should be level with the solar plexus

05:13.760 --> 05:20.080
and the location of your fist should coincide with the central line. When initiating the move,

05:20.080 --> 05:25.600
the hand of your supporting arm should be slightly straightened at the level of the solar plexus

05:25.600 --> 05:31.120
and the hand of the defending arm should protect inwardly. The beginning position is about a fist's

05:31.120 --> 05:36.480
length above the shoulder. The elbow of your defending arm should defend by pulling towards

05:36.480 --> 05:42.240
the torso and the elbow of the supporting arm should be pulled slightly, brushing your waist

05:42.240 --> 05:49.440
at the same time. Uryomaki is a technique used to protect from an opponent's attack towards the face

05:49.440 --> 05:55.440
by raising your outer wrist. Cross your arms in front of your solar plexus and protect from

05:55.440 --> 06:01.440
an opponent's attack by raising them upwards. The fist on the defending arm should be above

06:01.440 --> 06:07.360
the elbow. The arm should be tilted with a space about the size of a fist between the back of the

06:07.360 --> 06:13.920
wrist and the forehead. Be careful not to raise your arm excessively, leaving space between the

06:13.920 --> 06:21.840
head and arms when seen from the front. Uptuggy is a technique used to kick towards a front target

06:21.840 --> 06:28.800
in a straight line. The kick should be made with your leg bent so that the kicking leg starts on

06:28.800 --> 06:35.120
top of the knee of your supporting leg. The forward kicking leg should be bent in a way where your

06:35.120 --> 06:40.560
front sole touches the ground first and your breathing and moves should complete when your

06:40.560 --> 06:47.520
rear sole touches the ground. Train this technique using coordination of the upper and lower body.

06:47.520 --> 06:55.520
Naranhisogi consists of keeping your feet parallel so that the back of the foot blades face each other,

06:56.160 --> 07:02.160
straightening your knees and stand naturally as if stopping after walking. The center of

07:02.160 --> 07:08.320
your body should be placed in between your feet, the chest relaxed. The distance between your feet

07:08.320 --> 07:14.320
for Apsogi is about one step. The center of your body should be placed between your feet

07:14.320 --> 07:18.960
and your body should stand straight. The back of the foot blade on the front foot

07:18.960 --> 07:24.080
and the inner part of the heel on the hind foot should be on the same line and the sole of the

07:24.080 --> 07:30.560
hind foot should face outwards with a tilt of around 30 degrees. Uptuggy is a standing posture

07:30.560 --> 07:36.640
where the knee on the front leg is bent and the two legs are spread apart front to back. The

07:36.640 --> 07:42.400
distance between the front and hind foot should be about one and a half steps and the space between

07:42.400 --> 07:48.320
the two feet should be about the length of a fist. The knee and foot of the front leg should face

07:48.320 --> 07:53.760
forward and the sole of the hind foot should face outwards with a tilt of around 30 degrees.

07:54.640 --> 07:59.600
The knee of your front leg should not bend excessively nor should it be sticking out in

07:59.600 --> 08:05.680
front of the tip of the foot. Your hind leg should straighten in a straight line. These are the basics

08:05.680 --> 08:12.960
to the nine key moves of Taeguk Iljang. We will now look at the nine moves in succession,

08:12.960 --> 08:20.320
connecting each move. The key moves of Taeguk Iljang first part consist of Wen Are Maki,

08:20.320 --> 08:28.080
Oren Jirugi, Oren Are Maki, Wen Jirugi, Wen Are Maki, and Oren Jirugi.

08:28.800 --> 08:34.320
Shift your weight to the sole of your right foot. Move your left foot lightly,

08:36.400 --> 08:40.720
brushing the ground so the sole of the left foot touches the ground before the other.

08:43.120 --> 08:48.720
Your breathing should be identical to that of Maki, the movement your rear sole touches the ground,

08:48.720 --> 08:55.040
completing the move. Nediki should be performed softly, naturally with two knees.

08:58.960 --> 09:05.920
Backward Doradiki from Oren Apsugi. Oren Jirugi should be performed with your weight on the sole

09:05.920 --> 09:14.240
of the left foot. The heel of the right foot should not be above the ground when performing Wen

09:14.240 --> 09:23.120
Apgubi Neryo Maki. Cautions for Taeguk Iljang. First part, taking a closer look.

09:23.920 --> 09:29.280
When performing Jirugi, be careful not to pull back the shoulder of the supporting arm.

09:31.280 --> 09:36.560
When performing Nediki, be careful not to let the rear sole touch the ground first.

09:37.600 --> 09:44.560
When performing Are Maki, be careful not to bend your elbows excessively and naturally connect to Jirugi.

09:44.560 --> 09:52.000
We will now practice the moves of Taeguk Iljang. First part in succession, slowly while watching the video.

10:15.360 --> 10:24.400
Taeguk Iljang. Second part consists of Wen An Maki, Oren Jirugi, Oren An Maki, Wen Jirugi,

10:25.520 --> 10:28.640
Oren Are Maki, and Wen Jirugi.

10:31.920 --> 10:38.560
When connecting to Oren Apsugi and Wen An Maki from the previous move, push on the ground with

10:38.560 --> 10:44.880
your right foot and shift your weight to the left foot. Then perform Wen An Maki by pulling your right

10:44.880 --> 10:52.960
foot. Then when performing the Wen Apsugi and Oren Jirugi, set your feet and release the tension

10:52.960 --> 10:59.520
in the defending arm and connect to Jirugi. It is important to maintain equal tension on both arms

10:59.520 --> 11:06.400
when performing Jirugi and refrain from exerting unnecessary force. Then place weight on the sole

11:06.400 --> 11:11.760
of the right foot, turn and set, move your left arm facing the direction of progress,

11:11.760 --> 11:17.840
and perform the preparatory An Maki move with your right arm. Complete An Maki the moment the

11:17.840 --> 11:24.480
rear sole of the left foot touches the ground. Cautions for Taeguk Iljang. Second part, taking

11:24.480 --> 11:32.720
a closer look. When performing Oren Apsugi, Wen An Maki, do not put your feet together and perform

11:32.720 --> 11:41.600
Wen Apsugi and Wen An Maki. Your elbow should not move away from the body when performing An Maki.

11:43.040 --> 11:48.320
This is in order to properly transfer the energy from the spinning of the torso to the arm.

11:50.240 --> 11:52.960
We will now practice the moves of Taeguk Iljang.

11:53.600 --> 11:57.040
Second part in succession slowly while watching the video.

12:08.560 --> 12:15.440
The key moves of Taeguk Iljang. Third part consist of Wen Olgul Maki, Oren Apsugi,

12:15.440 --> 12:21.200
and Oren Jirugi. Oren Olgul Maki, Wen Apsugi, and Wen Jirugi.

12:23.680 --> 12:29.200
When performing Olgul Maki, protect the face by crossing your defending arm from under your

12:29.200 --> 12:36.960
supporting arm. The fist on the defending arm should be raised first, then move your hand

12:36.960 --> 12:43.920
slightly up to face level and defend by snapping the elbow up. Your other fist should be pulled to

12:43.920 --> 12:51.280
the waist and aligned with the center line of the wrist protecting the face. The space between the

12:51.280 --> 12:56.240
back of the wrist of the defending arm and forehead should be about the size of a fist,

12:56.880 --> 13:00.080
and the elbow above the fist should be tilted downwards.

13:03.360 --> 13:07.680
Cautions for Taeguk Iljang. Third part, taking a closer look.

13:08.640 --> 13:12.400
Be careful not to let the elbow of the defending arm fall back.

13:14.720 --> 13:21.040
We will now practice the moves of Taeguk Iljang. Third part in succession slowly while watching

13:21.040 --> 13:39.120
the video. The key moves of Taeguk Iljang fourth part consist of Wen Are Maki and Oren Jirugi.

13:40.720 --> 13:45.600
Push on the ground following the previous move. Place weight on the right foot.

13:45.600 --> 13:48.640
Turn and set your left foot 90 degrees inwardly.

13:51.520 --> 13:54.400
Perform the Wen Apgubi and Wen Are Maki.

13:56.480 --> 14:01.760
Set your foot for Apgubi Seogi and shout with Oren Jirugi simultaneously.

14:06.400 --> 14:10.240
Place weight on the right foot. Turn and set Baro.

14:13.120 --> 14:17.600
Cautions for Taeguk Iljang. Fourth part, taking a closer look.

14:17.600 --> 14:22.240
When setting your foot from Apgubi Seogi to the next Apgubi Seogi,

14:22.240 --> 14:24.400
be careful not to let your height change.

14:25.920 --> 14:30.800
Also, be careful not to perform multiple moves when turning to Baro.

14:33.200 --> 14:36.160
We will now practice the moves of Taeguk Iljang

14:36.160 --> 14:39.600
fourth part in succession slowly while watching the video.

14:47.760 --> 14:50.320
This concludes training for Taeguk Iljang.

14:51.440 --> 14:56.800
We will take another look at all the moves and master Pumse.

15:17.600 --> 15:43.440
We have looked at the key moves of Taeguk Iljang and provided thorough explanations

15:43.440 --> 15:46.000
for each move and things to watch out for.

15:46.960 --> 15:49.680
As a new initiate in the art of Taekwondo,

15:50.320 --> 15:56.720
we urge you to maintain your passion until the end, continuously and repeatedly honing your skills.

15:57.920 --> 16:00.720
We'll see you again in the Taeguk Iljang video.

16:01.440 --> 16:13.840
Thanks for watching.

